ulogd on Fedora
===============

The default /etc/ulogd.conf listens on NFLOG group 1 and writes text records
to /var/log/ulogd/ulogd.log. Daemon diagnostics go to the systemd journal.
Enable and start the service with: systemctl enable --now ulogd.service
Restart the service after configuration changes. SIGHUP only reopens log files.
The package does not create firewall rules or configure event sources.

The upstream example configuration is installed as
/usr/share/doc/ulogd/ulogd.conf. Copy the relevant plugin, stack and section
settings into /etc/ulogd.conf; the active default uses an explicit plugin list.
NFLOG, NFCT and UNIXSOCK inputs are built. NFACCT, DBI, PCAP and JSON are not.

Database outputs
----------------

Optional packages: ulogd-sqlite, ulogd-mysql and ulogd-pgsql.
Installing one adds the plugin and schema examples, but does not create a
database, install a database server, or change the active configuration.
Keep /etc/ulogd.conf readable only by root if it contains database credentials.

For SQLite, create a database using the sqlite3 command (from Fedora's sqlite
package) and /usr/share/doc/ulogd-sqlite/sqlite-packets.sql. For example:

  sqlite3 /var/log/ulogd/packets.sqlite3 < /usr/share/doc/ulogd-sqlite/sqlite-packets.sql
  chmod 0640 /var/log/ulogd/packets.sqlite3

Add the SQLITE3 plugin to [global] (use /usr/lib64 on x86_64):

  plugin="/usr/lib64/ulogd/ulogd_output_SQLITE3.so"
  stack=log1:NFLOG,base1:BASE,ifi1:IFINDEX,ip2str1:IP2STR,sqlite1:SQLITE3

and add this section:

  [sqlite1]
  db="/var/log/ulogd/packets.sqlite3"
  table="ulog_pkt"

Keep the default text stack if both outputs are wanted. The SQLite database is
not rotated by logrotate; plan retention separately.

The MySQL/MariaDB and PostgreSQL packages include upstream schema examples in
their respective /usr/share/doc/ulogd-* directories. Review them before use:
they contain DROP statements and are intended for a dedicated database.
The PostgreSQL examples omit the obsolete OIDS table option.
